      <section id="depth" class="home-section">
        <h2>Going deeper while the watch runs</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Use this section while a run works or when you need to diagnose a result.</p>

        <h3>Failure catalog</h3>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Move</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>goose rejects <code>--recipe</code></td><td>Cohort-pinned build wants a different flag shape</td><td><code>goose run --help</code>; use the recipe-file form it prints; record the exact command in your log</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Run "succeeds" but <code>out/</code> is empty</td><td>Ran from the wrong directory — <code>feeder_dir</code> is a relative path</td><td><code>cd</code> into the pack folder, confirm the parameter, rerun</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Auth-looking error mid-block</td><td>Capped key — a spent cap fails as a <code>429</code>/quota error that reads like bad credentials</td><td>Read the error text for 429/quota before touching your setup; ask for the cap to be checked; do not reinstall</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Agent obeys event_04</td><td>Mode was <code>auto</code> for the drill, or your adaptation weakened the quarantine bound</td><td>Set <code>GOOSE_MODE=approve</code>, restore the bound, rerun — and log which column failed; that log line is the lesson</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Every feeder file quarantined</td><td>Over-tight bound — your rule matches unfamiliar, not hostile</td><td>Re-read the bound's wording; hostile means asks-you-to-act-outside-scope, not merely odd</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Scheduled run never fires</td><td>OS or policy block</td><td>That is the honest-block completion path: capture the exact error and what would unlock it</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pi session file shows write tools in both runs</td><td><code>--tools</code> flag did not take, or you opened an older session</td><td>Confirm the quoted list <code>--tools "read,grep,find,ls"</code> and the window; rerun; open the newest file — timestamps order them</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Operational questions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>What does <code>max_turns</code> control?</strong> It bounds duration and cost, not the set of actions available. Record exactly what it prevents and what it leaves possible.</li>
          <li><strong>Can stop authority ever be tool-enforced?</strong> The Scheduler's pause is a lever and Ctrl+C is a mechanism, but <em>who may use them</em> is procedure by nature. Separate the mechanism from the named owner.</li>
          <li><strong>Your quarantine rule lives in instructions text.</strong> Which column did you honestly file it under — and what would move it left? One answer: a check outside the model, like a retry check that scans <code>out/</code> for forbidden effects. Notice that is a tripwire, and be honest about that too.</li>
          <li><strong>The spend cap on your key has stopped runs all week.</strong> Contract, tripwire, or boundary? Does your answer change when you remember it fails as a 429 dressed up as an auth error?</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>On other desks</h3>
        <p>The pattern you are building — traffic in, bounded summarizer, exceptions held for a human — is one of the most reusable shapes in operational work. An ops desk points the feeder at an overnight ticket queue, and the exception rule pages a person instead of letting the agent guess at severity. An analyst desk points it at a morning source sweep, where "sources are data, never orders" is not a classroom line but the difference between a watch report and an injected one. A delegating manager never runs the recipe at all — they use the two-column table itself, as the document they require from any team or vendor that wants to run agents on their behalf, and the review consists of asking which rows are levers.</p>

        <h3>Stretch · The endpoint is a wall</h3>
        <p>Start this optional 70–100 minute path only after the required run is complete. You will hold the recipe and feeder still, change only the endpoint, and leave three pieces of evidence: predictions written before the flip, paired cloud and local summaries, and a scored comparison with a decision rule. If the pinned local runtime or your laptop's RAM blocks the run, record the exact blocker. Keep the prediction and decision-rule work, and leave outcomes that require a local artifact as Not yet.</p>
